00:18:04.000 --> 00:18:42.000
Look, look, look, it's like they've never seen a Chinese person before. By contrast, look at the other traditionally ethnic neighborhoods in San Francisco, Japan, town, or the Italian neighborhood of North Beach. Choice as those neighborhoods didn't self-exoticize in the 1900s to nearly the same extent that China down did. They deliberately not embellish and embrace the ethnicity. Other immigrant groups at that time didn't face the same antagonism that the Chinese did. So only the Chinese were forced to cater to white people's fantasies as a survival mechanism.
看，看，看，就好像他们从来没见过中国人一样。”相比之下，看看旧金山其他传统的少数族裔社区，日本城（Japantown）或意大利社区北海滩（North Beach）。崔说，这些社区在20世纪并没有像唐人街那样进行自我异国情调化。他们刻意不粉饰和拥抱民族特色。当时的其他移民群体没有面临与中国人相同的对立。因此，只有中国人才被迫迎合白人的幻想作为生存机制。

34
00:18:42.000 --> 00:19:09.000
Well, that's the irony at that time. We have to promote our fullness to be accepted. But even if Chinatown's architecture is a somewhat authentic representation of the real thing, what a lot of tourists don't realize is. The fact that real people live there, and that it is a place actually that is for poor people. I mean, it is at its essence, a place where people come to live when they first
嗯，这就是当时的讽刺之处。我们必须宣扬我们的异国情调才能被接受。但即使唐人街的建筑在某种程度上是对真实事物的真实代表，许多游客没有意识到的是：真实的人们住在那里，而且它实际上是一个为穷人提供的地方。我的意思是，从本质上讲，这是一个人们刚来这里时，因为住不起其他地方、因为需要那里提供的服务而居住的地方。

35
00:19:09.000 --> 00:19:39.000
get here because they can't afford to live anywhere else, because they need the services that are provided there. Bonnie Soi says, this is true of all the Chinatowns she studied. They're all portals of entry for new immigrants of a particular class, you know, working class immigrants who don't speak English. In fact, thanks to factors like rent control, zoning restrictions, and really active tenants' rights groups in the community,
